"Touch your forehead. It is warm. Touch it on a cold winter morning, and it is still warm. A rock feels cold in winter and hot in summer, but you stay almost the same all year: close to 37 degrees Celsius. How does your body do that? First, where does the warmth come from? In the last lesson, you learned that your cells burn sugar to get energy. That slow burning also gives off heat. Most of your heat is made deep inside, in organs such as your liver, brain, and heart, and in your muscles when they work. Your blood picks up the heat and carries it around your body, like warm water in a heating pipe. A small part deep in your brain works like the thermostat of an air conditioner. It checks the temperature of your blood and gets messages from your skin. If you are a little too hot or too cold, it sends out orders. When you are too hot, two things happen. The blood vessels in your skin open wider, so more warm blood flows near the surface and gives its heat to the air. That is why your face turns red. And your sweat glands push water onto your skin. As the sweat dries, it takes heat away with it. You learned about sweat in the lesson about skin: it is your body's own cooling system. When you are too cold, the opposite happens. The blood vessels in your skin narrow, and warm blood stays deep inside to protect your organs. That is why your fingers and toes get cold first. Tiny muscles pull your hairs upright and make goose bumps. If that is not enough, your muscles start to shiver: they tighten and relax very fast, and that makes heat. Sometimes, when you are sick, your body turns its own thermostat up. That is a fever. As the fever starts, 37 degrees suddenly feels too cold, so you shiver under a blanket even though your skin is hot. When the thermostat goes back to normal, you feel hot and you sweat. Your temperature also changes a little every day, even when you are well: it is lowest in the early morning and highest in the late afternoon. Keep a temperature diary with the tool below the model, and see your own daily wave.": "0d3c47e67b95",
"We began to shiver in the cold wind.": "731ece9d4149",
"What two things does your body do when it is too hot?": "18402540bb75",
"When you are too cold, the opposite happens. The blood vessels in your skin narrow, and warm blood stays deep inside to protect your organs. That is why your fingers and toes get cold first. Tiny muscles pull your hairs upright and make goose bumps. If that is not enough, your muscles start to shiver: they tighten and relax very fast, and that makes heat.": "eb5f9d846637",
